# Rahu-Man

## Lore

The Rahu-Men are an ancient race of four-armed giants of the Palladium World who once ranked among its greatest warriors, Mind Mages, wizards and scholars; even the Titans respected them. Unable to adapt as the world changed, feared by humans and non-humans alike, they were hunted - above all by a failed Western Empire campaign of extermination - and the survivors withdrew to the highest peaks of the Old Kingdom Mountains to live as scholars, mystics and shepherds, fostering the belief that they are extinct. Both sexes exist in equal numbers; "Rahu-man" is simply the race''s name.

Some have taken to dimensional travel. A community of nearly 600 has lived in the Himalayas of Earth for over four thousand years - glimpses of these giants in white sheepskins gave rise to the Abominable Snowman. On Rifts Earth a few fought in the Tolkeen conflict, some are Cyber-Knights or ride with them, and others turn up at Lazlo, the Magic Zone, England, India, Germany, Russia and China. General Rasheen of the New German Republic''s Council of Governors is a Rahu-Man, a 9th level Cyber-Knight with one bionic arm. The Phoenix Empire captures and kills or sells to Atlantis any it finds.

**Alignment:** Any.

**Height:** 12 to 15 feet (3.6 to 4.6 m). **Weight:** 350 to 500 pounds.

**Average Life Span:** 1,000 years.

## GM Notes

Rahu-Men are gentle, intellectual and respectful of all peoples, yet love physical sport and contests, so most are trained and deadly fighters. Travellers into hostile country wear custom body armor costing 10 to 20 times the human-sized price, with 250 to 500 M.D.C. and sometimes magic; they favor pole arms, forks and the ball and chain, and among modern weapons Techno-Wizard weapons, Vibro-Blades, precision lasers and heavy weapons, particle beams and plasma first. Young Rahu-Men of 1st to 3rd level are far keener on travel than their elders. Enemies are most other giants, Ogres and Trolls, and they distrust humans and Elves; Titans and Kobolds are their traditional allies. The ritual eating of a worthy foe is an honor in their eyes, not predation - but an insane Rahu-Man can be a horrific monster.
